-- Passage wg12-paragraph-transitions-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('169c0539-07a7-5d55-94db-fa3291c96318', 'wg12-paragraph-transitions-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-paragraph-transitions'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Charting the Ocean Floor$q$, $q$(1) Until the twentieth century, sailors who wanted to know the depth of the sea relied on a weighted rope. (2) They lowered it over the side until it struck bottom, then measured how much line had been let out. (3) The method was slow, and in deep water a single reading could take hours. (4) ____, the ocean floor remained largely a mystery, and many people imagined it as a flat, featureless plain.

(5) Echo sounding changed that picture. (6) A ship sends a pulse of sound downward and records how long the echo takes to return from the seafloor. (7) Since sound travels through seawater at a fairly steady speed, the delay reveals the depth. (8) Ships could now record thousands of soundings in a single voyage, ____ the rope had produced only a handful.

(9) When researchers plotted these measurements, they discovered a landscape more dramatic than anything on land. (10) Long mountain ranges wind through the middle of the oceans, and some trenches plunge deeper than the tallest peaks rise. (11) Some fish that live near the bottom produce their own light. (12) The mapmakers who drew the first detailed charts had to join scattered readings into continuous shapes, a task that required careful calculation as well as informed judgment.

(13) Even with modern instruments that sweep a wide strip of seafloor at once, only a portion of the ocean floor has been mapped in fine detail. (14) The deep sea, in other words, still holds plenty of territory for future explorers to chart.$q$, 'draft')
