   $q$Building a town's seawall took two years, and repairing it after a single winter storm took nearly as long. The writer wants one balanced sentence that highlights this contrast. Which choice does this best?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The seawall took two years to build and nearly as long to repair after a single winter storm.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The seawall took two years to build, but a winter storm destroyed it completely in one night.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"adds-new-fact"},{"text":"The seawall, which took two years to build, was damaged by a winter storm, and repairs took a while.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"vague-wording"},{"text":"Taking two years to build and repaired after a winter storm, the seawall needed nearly as long.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"faulty-parallelism"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The seawall took two years to build and nearly as long to repair after a single winter storm.$q$, $q$The paired phrases "two years to build" and "nearly as long to repair" mirror each other, so the contrast stands out. The choice ending "repairs took a while" keeps the facts but blurs the comparison with the vague "took a while."$q$, 'draft', null)
